  clock: 3  # 8c51
  nonce: 8c51  # 8c51
  message: "SATISFIED by PR #293 (merged, dev 0ca355eddb). Behavioural evidence below, not a\nproxy metric.\n\nFILED REPRO, RE-RUN AGAINST THE MERGED TREE — all three arms now carry the import\npath where two of them previously carried the `external` placeholder:\n\n  ginHandler   -> go:github.com/gin-gonic/gin:0-0:JSON        (was already OK)\n  echoHandler  -> go:github.com/labstack/echo/v4:0-0:JSON     (was go:external)\n  fiberListen  -> go:github.com/gofiber/fiber/v2:0-0:Listen   (was go:external)\n\nFIX. `_go_package_identifier` (go.py) replaces the bare\n`import_path.rsplit(\"/\", 1)[-1]`. It strips a trailing Go-modules major-version\nelement (N >= 2 only, because v0/v1 modules carry no suffix so a literal `/v1`\nelement is more likely a real directory) and, separately, gopkg.in's dotted\nspelling (`gopkg.in/yaml.v2` -> `yaml`, and gopkg.in genuinely does use `.v1`).\nBoth patterns are anchored and digits-only; the path-element rule refuses to\nstrip with no earlier element to fall back to; the dotted rule is scoped to the\ngopkg.in host. 22 tests, including an adversarial guard that `v2`, `v2beta1`,\n`v1alpha1`, `v2bar` and `github.com/foo/yaml.v2` are NOT stripped — over-stripping\nwould have invented a defect while closing one.\n\nDIRECTION, MEASURED ON 13 REAL GO REPOS, BOTH SIGNS\n(scripts/measure-go-import-versioning.py, in-repo, arms in separate interpreters\nso no module-level cache is shared between them):\n\n  calls edges                377,503 -> 377,503   IDENTICAL IN EVERY REPO\n  dsts with 'external' slot   83,267 ->  81,974   (-1,293, 1.55%)\n  gained dsts                                       1,446\n    recovered an import path                        1,289\n    resolved to an in-repo symbol                     157\n  REGRESSIONS                                           0\n\n2 of 13 repos changed by exactly zero (cosign, gocryptfs). That null is only\nreportable because the positive control fires: a synthetic /v2 fixture moves 1\nsite in the same instrument.\n\nTHE IN-REPO BUCKET IS REPORTED SEPARATELY, never folded into the headline, because\nthe prior Go typing attempt moved 692 edges and put 118 (17.1%) on the WRONG\nin-repo symbol. The 157 landings here are type 84, function 62, method 9, struct 1,\nfield 1. The 84 `type` landings are Go type CONVERSIONS (`caddy.Duration(x)`),\ncalls only syntactically — that edge was ALREADY a `calls` edge before this change,\npointing at `external`. This makes its target accurate and thereby makes a\npre-existing modelling question visible; that question is WI-jalal's family (a\ncalls edge whose dst is not callable), not this item's.\n\nTHE INSTRUMENT HAD TWO DEFECTS OF ITS OWN, both caught by its own controls and both\nfixed before any number above was believed. Recording them because both are\ninstances of rules already on the board and both would otherwise be rediscovered:\n  - it keyed each call site on (src, line, dst NAME slot) — a discriminator derived\n    from the thing under test, since resolving a dst in-repo can re-spell that slot\n    receiver-qualified — and duly reported a non-destruction failure that was its\n    own;\n  - it then paired the two arms' dsts POSITIONALLY within a site, silently matching\n    UNRELATED calls on one line whenever sort order shifted, manufacturing 5\n    regressions that do not exist (`BlobStatter.Stat -> external:Digest` is two\n    different calls).\nBoth are now a multiset difference over a caller-side-only key, needing no pairing.\n\nNO CROSS-LANGUAGE PARITY TEST, and the reason is recorded rather than left silent.\nThe project rule is that unavoidable per-language logic ships a parity test over the\nlive registry. This is not merely per-language, it is per-language SEMANTICS: /vN is\na Go module convention and gopkg.in a Go hosting convention, so asserting either for\npython or rust would assert something false. WHAT DOES GENERALISE IS THE SHAPE — \"an\nidentifier derived from a path segment can be defeated by a version marker occupying\nthat segment\" — and that is an OPEN QUESTION for the other analyzers, not an\nassertion about them. No other analyzer was read."  # 8c51
